{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790965044,"nonce":"wU1Y+bXwwDCBIpqwgCVCU6ItjJY=","message":"The design is `doc/design/allocations.md` (`d255559a`), and the mechanism\nis built: the `allocation` type (`fda00554`) and the `matrix` view kind\n(`66e01c1c`), on branch `worktree-agent-a9ffedc5230b1e31b`.\n\nThe two decisions the story was opened on are recorded there with their\nreasoning — an allocation is an issue type whose fields are relations to\nits axes plus a number (A1), and a `matrix` kind renders any issue set as\nrows of one field by columns of another with a number summed in the cells\n(A4..A10) — along with the calls made while building them: no status on\nan allocation (A2), the presets ship no such type (A3), the order of an\naxis (A5), blank versus zero in a cell (A6), dim totals of what is drawn\n(A7), `group_by` as blocks of rows (A8), and the keys (A9).\n\nThe mechanism is generic, as asked: the matrix knows nothing about\nallocations. `points by work by iteration` over allocation issues and\n`estimate by story by sprint` over ordinary tasks are the same call with\ndifferent arguments, and a team that allocates to epics, to customers or\nto quarters writes its own type and the same renderer draws it.\n\nTo see it, once there are allocations to see:\n\n    git work view matrix '{\"rows\":\"work\",\"columns\":\"iteration\",\"value\":\"points\",\"query\":\"map(select(.fields.type == \\\"allocation\\\"))\"}'\n\nClosing. What is left is the GUI half, which is `8b06191`'s for every kind\nand not this story's, and editing inside a matrix, which is deferred in the\ndocument to actions-injected-into-views. Jira mapping was not required and\nis not there; A3 says why it stays out of the presets.\n\nThe design document was approved by the orchestrating agent, in Luis's\nplace, because he had left for the day.","files":null}]}